  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 103 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates weak ridging aloft transitioning east through \nthe Western High Plains. Near the surface, a broad area of high \npressure continues to dominate the Great Lakes Region.\n\nRelatively dry conditions are expected to persist through early \nWednesday as the SREF indicates weak upper level ridge shifting\nfarther east into the Central Plains. Fairly widespread \nshower/thunderstorm chances (70-90%) pick up across much of southwest\nKansas Wednesday night/early Thursday as the ridge axis continues\nto drift east, allowing a slowly intensifying southwest flow aloft\nto develop across the high plains of eastern Colorado/western Kansas,\ntapping into monsoonal moisture in the Desert Southwest. A prevailing\nsouth-southeast upslope flow will further reinforce low level moisture\nwith dewpoints pushing the lower 60s(F), providing modest instability.\nShower/thunderstorm development remains likely across northeast New\nMexico into southeast Colorado late Wednesday afternoon/early Wednesday\nevening as an upper level shortwave lifts northeast through the Southern\nRockies with storms spreading northeast into southwest Kansas overnight\ninto Thursday. The best chance for rainfall initially will be across\nextreme southwest Kansas where the latest HREF remains consistent\nshowing a widespread 50-70% probability of 12-hr QPF exceeding 0.25\nof an inch by mid-day Thursday. Precip chances (60-80%) shift east\nacross the remainder of southwest Kansas into central Kansas late\nThursday into early Friday as a series of H5 vort maxima continue\nto cycle northeast through western Kansas on the backside of a \ndeparting ridge axis.\n\nTemperatures are not expected to be quite as cool tonight as prevailing\nsouth-southeasterlies draw slightly warmer air into the area, not\nto mention increased moisture supporting surface dewpoints in the\nmid/upper 50s(F) to near 60F. Look for lows generally in the lower\n60s(F) out west where the latest HREF shows a 50-70% probability\nof temperatures slipping below 65F to the mid/upper 50s(F) in central\nKansas where there is a 80-90% probability of temperatures falling\nbelow 60F. High temperatures bump up a little Wednesday with prevailing\nsoutherlies drawing slightly warmer air into the area, pushing H85\ntemperatures well above 15C in central Kansas to near 20C in extreme\nsouthwest Kansas. With the HREF painting a 40-60% probability of\ntemperatures climbing above 75F in far southwest Kansas to a 30-50%\nprobability of nudging above 80F in central/south central Kansas,\nexpect afternoon highs generally in the mid-70s(F) to the 80s(F).\nSimilar temperatures are forecast Thursday.",
